THE CASE AGAINST THE TARGET:
The target mischaracterizes the typical obstruction. It claims typical stable mixed configurations are perfect matching cuts: remove a perfect matching M and color the resulting cycles. But stable mixed configurations correspond to any matching boundary M, not only perfect matchings. For a boundary matching of size αn, the expected number of stable configurations is exp(n h(α)) up to polynomial factors, with h(α) = -α log α - (1-2α)log(1-2α) - α - α log 2 + 2α log 3 - 1.5 log 3 + ((3-2α)/2)log(3-2α) - (3-2α)/2 + 1.5. This h is maximized at α=0.3, where h(0.3)≈0.58, while h(0.5)=log(2/√3)≈0.14. Thus perfect-matching cuts are exponentially subdominant; the typical obstruction has a boundary matching of size about 0.3n, and G-M is not a disjoint union of cycles but has many degree-3 vertices. This mischaracterizes the finite obstruction structures the problem asks for.

